#!/usr/bin/env bash
#MISE hide=true
# Refuse to start a task whose tools this machine does not have.
#
#     "$(dirname "$0")/_require" docker kubectl
#
# Put it directly under `set -euo pipefail`, before any work. Exits 0 when
# every named command is on PATH, otherwise prints what is missing, where you
# are, and what to do instead, and exits 1.
#
# The point is failing at the boundary rather than past it. A `[human]`
# description tag tells an agent not to try and does nothing about a task that
# runs anyway, half-works, and exits 0 — which is the failure mode that
# actually costs a debugging session. A description is also invisible at the
# moment of failure; this is not.
#
# Guard on what mise cannot supply. `mise.toml` installs dagger, pulumi,
# ansible-core and flyctl into `~/.local/share/mise`, so inside `mise run`
# those are on PATH even in the agent pod — guarding them would be a guard
# that never fires. What is genuinely absent there is the image's business:
# docker, kubectl, argocd, age, openssl, sqlite3.
#
# This checks PATH and nothing else. A task blocked by the blumeops vault or
# by the pod's lack of a tailnet route is not a case this catches — `op` and
# `ssh` already fail on their own, legibly, and a check that guessed at those
# would be the silent-degradation problem wearing a different hat.
#
# mise makes every file in mise-tasks/ a task, underscore or not, so
# `hide=true` above is what keeps it out of `mise tasks`. It still runs as
# `mise run _require`, which is a reasonable way to test it.
#
# The task name in the message comes from MISE_TASK_NAME, which mise exports
# and the calling task's shell passes down.
set -euo pipefail

missing=()
for tool in "$@"; do
    command -v "$tool" >/dev/null 2>&1 || missing+=("$tool")
done
[ ${#missing[@]} -eq 0 ] && exit 0

task="${MISE_TASK_NAME:-this task}"

# `agent` in a pod named agent-ws-* is the containerized Remote Control
# session — the one environment where the answer is "request it", not
# "install it".
in_pod=false
if [ "$(id -un)" = "agent" ]; then
    case "$(uname -n)" in
    agent-ws-*) in_pod=true ;;
    esac
fi

printf '%s needs: %s\n' "$task" "${missing[*]}" >&2

if $in_pod; then
    cat >&2 <<'EOF'

Not available in the agent pod: the agent-ws image carries a curated toolbox
and these are deliberately not in it. Nothing here degrades gracefully into a
useful result, so this stops rather than guessing.

What to do instead:
  - a privileged workflow covers it -> mise run request-run <workflow> <sha> \
        --pr <N> --why "…"   (Erich approves in Warrant, which dispatches it)
  - it does not -> it is a human job on gilbert; ask, and say what you needed
  - the tool belongs in the image -> that is a PR to containers/agent-ws/

See docs/explanation/warrant-approval-gated-runs.md and AGENTS.md
§Privileged actions.
EOF
else
    printf '\nInstall it, or run this from a host that has it (usually gilbert).\n' >&2
fi

exit 1
